# Build-context exclusions. This is the ONLY Docker ignore file, and it lives with the Dockerfile under
# docker/ (all Docker files stay in docker/, never in the project root). BuildKit — the default builder, and
# what the compose build uses — reads this `<Dockerfile>.dockerignore` automatically: compose sets
# `context: ..` (the project root) with `dockerfile: ./docker/Dockerfile`, so BuildKit pairs this file with
# that Dockerfile. (The deprecated non-BuildKit builder reads only a context-root `.dockerignore`, which we
# deliberately do not keep — build with BuildKit, the default since Docker 23.) Keep the image to CODE plus
# the shipped config TEMPLATES only — never a developer's runtime state or real secrets.

.git/
.vscode/

# Never copy the host's node_modules into the image: it may be built for a different OS/arch (e.g. macOS)
# and would break the Alpine runtime. The Dockerfile installs dependencies cleanly inside the image.
node_modules/

# Runtime / generated state. These are Docker named volumes at runtime, and a fresh volume seeds from the
# image on first run — so baking a developer's copy in would ship logs, backups, uploads and cached data
# into a brand-new install. Excluding them means a fresh volume starts empty and the app creates what it
# needs.
data/
logs/
backups/
uploads/
rollbacks/
temp/
downloads/
# Session secrets and live logins: a session file holds a per-instance signing secret and logged-in
# cookies. Baking them in would ship the developer's secret into the image and seed it into every fresh
# volume (a credential leak and cross-install auth bleed). A fresh volume must start empty; the app then
# generates its own secret.
sessions/

# Per-instance identity and any populated/real config. Build images from the shipped config TEMPLATES only —
# an app.json with an empty mongo_db_url, a credential-free bot.json, and (for a future Hub build) a hub.json
# template. Always build from a CLEAN checkout so those three files are the shipped templates, not your live
# config, or the image would carry real exchange keys and the app-password hash. The patterns below exclude
# the non-template files a working copy accumulates — per-host identity files (server.json, server-*.json),
# real-exchange bot configs (bot-*.json), and editor duplicates ("app copy.json") — while the canonical
# templates (app.json, bot.json, hub.json) are intentionally kept so a fresh install can seed from them.
config/server*.json
config/bot-*.json
config/*copy*.json
config/*real*.json
config/*REAL*.json

# Dev-only tooling and tests the runtime never needs (and which are not part of the deployed project).
# The test corpus lives under libs/test/, but its two runner scripts (run-tests.js, precheck.js) sit at the TOP
# of libs/test/ and are intentionally KEPT: run-tests.js exits gracefully when the corpus is absent and
# precheck.js syntax-checks the shipped libs/, so the package.json check/test scripts behave in the image
# instead of crashing on a missing file. Exclude the test SUBDIRECTORIES (the corpus) rather than all of
# libs/test/, which would take the runners with it.
.githooks/
libs/test/ai/
libs/test/app/
libs/test/queries/
libs/test/scheduledtasks/
libs/test/strategies/
libs/test/webserver/

# The local-only working backlog is never shipped.
symbot-backlog.md

# Editor / OS cruft that must never ship in the image.
**/.DS_Store
